-\abstract{\textbf{Introduction:} A neural maximum power point tracking algorithm combines current-characteristic identification, bounded online updates and load regulation in firmware targeting an ESP32. Its evaluation distinguishes prediction accuracy from energy tracking.\\
-\textbf{Materials and methods:} The algorithm uses a 2--16--1 current predictor, a two-stage search for the voltage reference and a bounded load correction. Its output layer is updated from measured current, with incremental conductance used outside the covered environmental domain. Frozen and adaptive networks were compared with perturb and observe, incremental conductance, and adaptive perturb and observe. Seven programmed profiles and ten paired noise seeds produced 350 host simulations. Eighteen supplied Wokwi logs were audited separately. A matched static pair assessed operation at 600 W/m$^2$ and 37 $^\circ$C. Unequal manual dynamic trajectories were retained for descriptive assessment.\\
-\textbf{Results:} Across five primary host profiles, mean model-referenced tracking efficiency was 99.429\% for the frozen network and 99.427\% with online updates. In the Wokwi static pair, ten updates occurred with learning enabled. Current prediction root mean square error during the final 30 s was 0.0135 A, compared with 0.1483 A without updates. Recorded energy over a common 120 s window was 26,547.51 and 26,548.38 J, respectively.\\
-\textbf{Conclusions:} The available runs show improved local current prediction without increased energy capture. Manual dynamic records show load recovery but do not provide matched energy contrasts. The voltage criterion was not met in the observed 45 and 50 $^\circ$C plateaus. The findings concern a digital plant and simulated firmware execution, not a physical photovoltaic converter.}
+\abstract{\textbf{Introduction:} A neural current model can improve local prediction without locating the maximum-power point more accurately. This study separates identification, reference generation and regulation in photovoltaic MPPT firmware targeting an ESP32.\\
+\textbf{Materials and methods:} Three versions of a 2--16--1 model were evaluated: current-only updating, constrained recalibration, and measurement-assisted power verification with guarded current/slope updates. An initial 350-run host campaign was followed by an 880-run matched comparison including P\&O, incremental conductance, adaptive P\&O and a sweep-calibrated affine reference. A further 60 runs tested passive verification. Twenty-nine Wokwi files containing 7491 complete observations were audited separately.\\
+\textbf{Results:} Across five primary profiles, constrained neural tracking reached 99.4302\% model-referenced efficiency, compared with 98.3843\% for the implemented INC and 99.4295\% for the affine comparator. Active power verification reached 99.4159\%, with no practical online-versus-frozen energy gain. In static host operation, measured-voltage standard deviation was approximately 0.032 V for calibrated neural references and 0.359--0.443 V for the classical branches. A matched static Wokwi pair showed current RMSE of 0.0135 A online versus 0.1483 A frozen, without increased energy. Equal-input replay reproduced lower current error alongside greater reference error.\\
+\textbf{Conclusions:} The calibrated-reference controllers reduced command activity and improved criterion-based load recovery for the tested tunings. Similar affine-reference performance prevents attributing their energy advantage to neural representation alone. The neural model adds current estimates and inspectable update diagnostics. Verification and bounded adaptation carry costs and do not establish physical-converter performance, global tracking, or energetic superiority of online learning.}
 \keyword{photovoltaic systems; maximum power point tracking; online learning; ESP32; digital photovoltaic model; measurement scaling}
