 \section{Conclusions}
-A neural MPPT algorithm combining bounded output-layer identification, predicted-power search, voltage-reference regulation and INC fallback was evaluated within an ESP32-oriented photovoltaic simulation. The completed static Wokwi pair shows reduced current prediction error after ten updates, while recorded energy is slightly lower than with frozen weights. The programmed host comparison likewise provides no energy benefit from the current update rule. These findings support local model adaptation but not improved energy capture. The manual dynamic records show load recovery by both variants and criterion non-attainment at the observed 45 and 50 $^\circ$C plateaus. Their unequal inputs do not identify a dynamic energy effect of learning. Verified matched dynamic Wokwi trials remain necessary to complete that comparison.
+A current-model MPPT design was evaluated through an initial controller, constrained calibration and a guarded power-verification extension. In the later matched host campaign, NN-C achieved 99.4302\% aggregate tracking efficiency over S0--S4, compared with 98.3843\% for the implemented INC branch. It also reduced measured steady-state voltage variation and command activity under the specified settings. The affine comparator reached 99.4295\% and reproduced those regulation benefits, showing that calibrated reference placement is sufficient to explain most of the advantage in this source family.
+
+The neural representation adds a current estimate and observable identification residuals. The matched static Wokwi pair and equal-input diagnostic replay show lower current error after parameter updates. Neither establishes an energy advantage from those updates. NN-V accepts bounded steps against current and power-slope criteria and records why proposals are admitted or rejected. Its active verification incurs an energy cost: aggregate efficiency is 99.4159\%, and its online--frozen energy contrast remains below the study's practical criterion. These results support a model-identification and verification capability, not universal superiority of neural or online control.
+
+The source is a single-maximum digital panel with a load-percentage actuator. The host comparison does not validate an ESP32 binary, converter dynamics, physical efficiency, fault recognition or transfer to the ten documentary commercial modules. The 29 Wokwi records retain their observed coverage and unmatched dynamic histories. A final physical-controller and matched dynamic assessment is needed before extending the claims beyond the reported simulation workflow.
 
